# PVS-Studio diagnostic configuration
# Docs: https://pvs-studio.com/en/docs/manual/6630/

# --- V1064: false positives on floating-point FE math ---------------------
# The finite-element templates are constrained with `std::floating_point T`,
# so expressions like `T{1} / T{3}` are FLOATING-POINT division (= 0.333...),
# not integer division. PVS-Studio's V1064 heuristic misreads the integer
# literals inside the braces and reports "result will always be zero".
#
# We suppress V1064 by MESSAGE substring: PVS pretty-prints the templated
# operand as 'T { n }', which appears only for these std::floating_point
# template false positives. A genuine integer-division-always-zero bug with
# concrete `int` literals produces a different message (e.g. "'1' operand ...")
# and is therefore still reported.
#
# Syntax: //-V::<code>::{<message-substring>}
# (verified locally with pvs-studio-analyzer 8.01: drops all 51 V1064 to 0).
#
# NOTE: for V1064 the message-based form is used rather than the line/source
# form //-V:T{:1064, because the `T{` source substring did not match in this
# analyzer flow. (The line form does work for other substrings — see V607.)
//-V::1064::{'T { }

# --- V607 "Ownerless expression" — false positives in test code -----------
# Unit tests deliberately construct objects inside lambdas to assert that
# construction throws, e.g.:
#     expect(throws([] { math_expression<T>{ "bad input" }; }));
#     expect(throws<std::invalid_argument>([] { ildlt_preconditioner{ ... }; }));
# PVS sees the constructed temporary as an expression with no effect, but the
# intended effect IS the exception. We suppress V607 only on source lines that
# contain `expect(throws`, which is exactly this test idiom. Verified locally
# with pvs-studio-analyzer 8.01: drops all 16 V607 to 0 while leaving V607
# active for any other ownerless expression.
#
# Syntax (line/source-substring form): //-V:<src-substring>:<code>
# (substring must contain no spaces).
//-V:expect(throws:607
